{"_id":"beast01","name":"Unicorn","category":"Beasts","text":"Unicorn (Unicornis) — A small but fierce one-horned beast that hunters cannot catch by force; it can only be taken when it lays its head in the lap of a maiden. Moral: Christ, who took on flesh through the Virgin Mary. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast140.htm"}
{"_id":"beast02","name":"Lion","category":"Beasts","text":"Lion (Leo) — The king of beasts. It wipes out its tracks with its tail, sleeps with its eyes open, and its cubs are born lifeless until their father breathes on them on the third day. Moral: Christ, who hid his divinity and rose on the third day. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast78.htm"}
{"_id":"beast03","name":"Elephant","category":"Beasts","text":"Elephant (Elephas) — A huge, long-lived animal with little desire to mate. It sleeps leaning on a tree; if the tree is cut it falls and cannot get up until a small elephant lifts it. Moral: Fallen humanity (Adam) raised up by Christ. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast77.htm"}
{"_id":"beast04","name":"Panther","category":"Beasts","text":"Panther (Panthera) — A gentle, many-coloured beast whose only enemy is the dragon. After eating it sleeps for three days, then wakes with a sweet breath that draws all other animals to it. Moral: The risen Christ, who draws all people to himself. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast79.htm"}
{"_id":"beast05","name":"Beaver","category":"Beasts","text":"Beaver (Castor) — Hunted for a part of its body used in medicine; when chased, it bites that part off and leaves it for the hunter so it can escape. Moral: A person who casts off their vices to escape the devil. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast152.htm"}
{"_id":"beast06","name":"Bonnacon","category":"Beasts","text":"Bonnacon (Bonasus) — A bull-like beast whose curled-back horns are useless for fighting, so it defends itself by spraying burning dung over a long distance at its pursuers. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast80.htm"}
{"_id":"beast07","name":"Hyena","category":"Beasts","text":"Hyena (Hyaena) — Lives among tombs and feeds on corpses. It was believed to change sex and to imitate human voices to lure people out at night. Moral: Unstable, double-minded people. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast153.htm"}
{"_id":"beast08","name":"Manticore","category":"Beasts","text":"Manticore (Manticora) — An Indian beast with a man's face, a lion's body, three rows of teeth and a scorpion-like tail. It has a piping voice and a hunger for human flesh. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast177.htm"}
{"_id":"beast09","name":"Yale","category":"Beasts","text":"Yale (Eale) — A dark beast with an elephant's tail and a boar's jaws. Its long horns can swivel, so it keeps one folded back as a spare while it fights with the other. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast142.htm"}
{"_id":"beast10","name":"Leucrota","category":"Beasts","text":"Leucrota (Leucrota) — A very swift Indian beast the size of an ass, with a lion's chest, a horse-like head, cloven hooves and a solid ridge of bone in place of teeth. It can mimic human speech. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast160.htm"}
{"_id":"beast11","name":"Centaur","category":"Beasts","text":"Centaur (Centaurus) — A creature that is human from the waist up and horse below. Moral: People who are two-faced, or whose reason is ruled by their animal nature. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast384.htm"}
{"_id":"beast12","name":"Griffin","category":"Beasts","text":"Griffin (Gryphes) — Has the body of a lion and the head and wings of an eagle. It lives in northern mountains and is strong enough to carry off a whole ox. Moral: Read sometimes as the devil and sometimes as Christ. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast151.htm"}
{"_id":"beast13","name":"Fox","category":"Beasts","text":"Fox (Vulpes) — A cunning trickster. It rolls in red mud and lies still as if dead, and when birds land on it to peck, it springs up and eats them. Moral: The devil, who seems harmless to those who live by the flesh. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast179.htm"}
{"_id":"beast14","name":"Wolf","category":"Beasts","text":"Wolf (Lupus) — A greedy predator whose strength lies in its chest and jaws. If it sees a man before the man sees it, the man loses his voice. Moral: The devil, who prowls around the fold of the faithful. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast180.htm"}
{"_id":"beast15","name":"Bear","category":"Beasts","text":"Bear (Ursus) — Its cubs are born as shapeless lumps of flesh, which the mother licks into shape. It heals itself by eating certain plants. Moral: Unformed souls given shape through teaching and care. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast171.htm"}
{"_id":"beast16","name":"Hedgehog","category":"Beasts","text":"Hedgehog (Ericius) — Covered in spines. At harvest time it rolls on fallen grapes or fruit so they stick to its quills, then carries them home to its young. Moral: The devil, who steals the spiritual fruit of careless souls. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast217.htm"}
{"_id":"beast17","name":"Weasel","category":"Beasts","text":"Weasel (Mustela) — Said to conceive through the ear and give birth through the mouth (or the reverse). It is the only creature that can kill the basilisk. Moral: People who hear the word of God but do not act on it. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast150.htm"}
{"_id":"beast18","name":"Antelope","category":"Beasts","text":"Antelope (Antalops) — A fierce, swift beast with long saw-like horns that can cut down trees. When it drinks from the Euphrates, its horns get tangled in thick bushes and hunters catch it. Moral: A person trapped by vices such as drunkenness and lust. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast137.htm"}
{"_id":"beast19","name":"Parandrus","category":"Beasts","text":"Parandrus (Parandrus) — An ox-sized beast from Ethiopia with branching antlers and a stag's head. It can change colour to blend in with whatever is near it. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast178.htm"}
{"_id":"beast20","name":"Satyr","category":"Beasts","text":"Satyr (Satyrus) — Described as a kind of ape with a lively, expressive face and restless movements. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast101074.htm"}
{"_id":"beast21","name":"Pelican","category":"Birds","text":"Pelican (Pelicanus) — Devoted to its young, but when the chicks strike their parent, it kills them. After three days the parent pierces its own breast and revives them with its blood. Moral: Christ, whose blood gives life to humanity. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast244.htm"}
{"_id":"beast22","name":"Phoenix","category":"Birds","text":"Phoenix (Fenix) — Only one exists at a time. Every five hundred years it builds a nest of spices, sets itself alight and rises again from the ashes. Moral: The resurrection of Christ. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast149.htm"}
{"_id":"beast23","name":"Caladrius","category":"Birds","text":"Caladrius (Caladrius) — A pure white bird kept in royal courts. If it looks at a sick person, they will recover as it draws the illness into itself and flies toward the sun; if it looks away, they will die. Moral: Christ, who takes away the sins of the world. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast143.htm"}
{"_id":"beast24","name":"Eagle","category":"Birds","text":"Eagle (Aquila) — When it grows old, it flies up toward the sun to burn away its dim sight and old feathers, then plunges three times into a spring and is made young again. Moral: Renewal of the soul through baptism. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast232.htm"}
{"_id":"beast25","name":"Owl","category":"Birds","text":"Owl (Bubo) — A night bird that shuns daylight, hides in dark ruins and is mobbed by other birds when it appears by day. Moral: Sinners who prefer darkness to the light of truth. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast245.htm"}
{"_id":"beast26","name":"Swan","category":"Birds","text":"Swan (Cygnus) — A white bird that sings most sweetly just before it dies. Its snow-white feathers were said to cover dark flesh. Moral: Hypocrisy: an attractive outside hiding sin within. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast237.htm"}
{"_id":"beast27","name":"Crane","category":"Birds","text":"Crane (Grus) — Cranes fly in ordered formations behind a leader. At night one stands guard holding a stone in its raised foot, so it wakes if it drops off to sleep. Moral: Watchfulness and order in the community of the faithful. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast234.htm"}
{"_id":"beast28","name":"Ostrich","category":"Birds","text":"Ostrich (Assida) — Has wings but cannot fly. It lays its eggs in the sand when a certain star rises and then forgets them, leaving the sun's warmth to hatch them. Moral: Forgetting earthly things to fix one's mind on heaven. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast238.htm"}
{"_id":"beast29","name":"Barnacle Goose","category":"Birds","text":"Barnacle Goose (Barliatus) — Grows on trees or driftwood beside the sea, hanging by its beak like fruit until it drops into the water and swims away. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast1195.htm"}
{"_id":"beast30","name":"Peacock","category":"Birds","text":"Peacock (Pavo) — Its flesh was thought never to rot. It shrieks in dismay when it looks down at its ugly feet. Moral: Pride, and the humility that comes from seeing one's faults. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast257.htm"}
{"_id":"beast31","name":"Siren","category":"Birds","text":"Siren (Siren) — A woman from the waist up, with the lower body of a bird or fish. It sings so sweetly that sailors fall asleep, and then it attacks them. Moral: The pleasures of the world that lull people into sin. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast246.htm"}
{"_id":"beast32","name":"Hoopoe","category":"Birds","text":"Hoopoe (Epopus) — When their parents grow old, young hoopoes pluck out the parents' worn feathers, keep them warm and feed them until they recover. Moral: The duty of children to care for their parents. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast243.htm"}
{"_id":"beast33","name":"Whale","category":"Fish","text":"Whale (Cetus) — So huge that sailors mistake its back for an island and light a fire on it; feeling the heat, it dives and drowns them. Its sweet breath draws small fish into its mouth. Moral: The devil, who deceives and drags souls down to hell. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast282.htm"}
{"_id":"beast34","name":"Dolphin","category":"Fish","text":"Dolphin (Delphinus) — Follows human voices and swims in schools. When dolphins leap and play in the waves, a storm is on its way. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast284.htm"}
{"_id":"beast35","name":"Sawfish","category":"Fish","text":"Sawfish (Serra) — A sea monster with huge saw-toothed fins. It races sailing ships, but soon tires and sinks back beneath the waves. Moral: People who start doing good but give up before the end. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast147.htm"}
{"_id":"beast36","name":"Echeneis","category":"Fish","text":"Echeneis (Echeneis) — A small fish only half a foot long that can hold a ship still in the water just by clinging to it. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast422.htm"}
{"_id":"beast37","name":"Mermaid","category":"Fish","text":"Mermaid (Sirena) — A sea creature that is a woman from the waist up and a fish below. Moral: Worldly temptation. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast283.htm"}
{"_id":"beast38","name":"Swordfish","category":"Fish","text":"Swordfish (Gladius) — Has a long, pointed, sword-like snout that it uses to pierce the hulls of ships and sink them. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast1966.htm"}
{"_id":"beast39","name":"Basilisk","category":"Serpents","text":"Basilisk (Basiliscus) — The king of serpents. It can kill with its gaze or breath and withers plants as it passes. Only the weasel can defeat it. Moral: The devil. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast265.htm"}
{"_id":"beast40","name":"Dragon","category":"Serpents","text":"Dragon (Draco) — The largest of all serpents. Its strength is in its tail, not its venom. It lies in wait on paths used by elephants, wraps its tail around their legs and suffocates them. Moral: The devil, who traps people along the paths of their lives. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast262.htm"}
{"_id":"beast41","name":"Amphisbaena","category":"Serpents","text":"Amphisbaena (Amphisbaena) — A serpent with a head at each end, so it can move in either direction. Its eyes shine like lamps and it is not harmed by cold. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast144.htm"}
{"_id":"beast42","name":"Salamander","category":"Serpents","text":"Salamander (Salamandra) — Can live in fire without being burned and can even put flames out. It is so poisonous that it can taint a whole well or a fruit tree. Moral: The righteous, who pass through fire unharmed. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast276.htm"}
{"_id":"beast43","name":"Asp","category":"Serpents","text":"Asp (Aspis) — When a snake charmer tries to lure it out, it presses one ear to the ground and plugs the other with its tail so it cannot hear the spell. Moral: People who block their ears to the word of God. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast268.htm"}
{"_id":"beast44","name":"Hydrus","category":"Serpents","text":"Hydrus (Hydrus) — Enemy of the crocodile. It covers itself in mud, slips into a sleeping crocodile's mouth and is swallowed, then bursts out of its belly alive. Moral: Christ descending into hell and rising victorious. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast272.htm"}
{"_id":"beast45","name":"Jaculus","category":"Serpents","text":"Jaculus (Jaculus) — A flying serpent that hides in trees and launches itself like a javelin at animals passing below. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast273.htm"}
{"_id":"beast46","name":"Mandrake","category":"Plants","text":"Mandrake (Mandragora) — A plant whose root looks like a human figure. Elephants eat it to awaken the desire to mate. Moral: The fall of Adam and Eve and the temptation that led to it. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast1098.htm"}
{"_id":"beast47","name":"Peridexion Tree","category":"Plants","text":"Peridexion Tree (Peridexion) — An Indian tree with sweet fruit where doves gather. The dragon fears its shadow, so the doves are safe as long as they stay in or near it. Moral: The faithful, who are safe from the devil while they stay close to Christ. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast263.htm"}
{"_id":"beast48","name":"Fire Stones","category":"Stones","text":"Fire Stones (Terrobuli) — A pair of stones, one male and one female. Kept apart they stay cool, but when brought together they burst into flame. Moral: Men and women should take care to avoid temptation. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast536.htm"}
{"_id":"beast49","name":"Ant","category":"Insects","text":"Ant (Formica) — Ants march in orderly lines and store grain in summer for the winter. They split each seed in two so it will not sprout, and they can tell wheat from barley by smell. Moral: Prudence, and telling the true meaning of scripture from false teaching. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast218.htm"}
{"_id":"beast50","name":"Bee","category":"Insects","text":"Bee (Apis) — Bees live in a well-ordered community under a king, share their work and gather honey from flowers. They were thought to be born from the carcasses of oxen. Moral: An ordered, hard-working Christian community. Read more: https://www.bestiary.ca/beasts/beast260.htm"}
